    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Marine collagen is type I collagen extracted from fish skin and scales, hydrolysed
        into short peptides so it can be absorbed.</strong> It is the source most used in skin
        formulas, and the pooled trial evidence (19 randomised trials, 1,125 people) shows better
        hydration and elasticity than placebo at doses of roughly 2.5g to 10g a day.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Type:</strong> almost entirely type I, the collagen that makes up the bulk of your dermis.</li>
        <li><strong>Peptide size:</strong> look for hydrolysed peptides under 2,000 Daltons. Above that, absorption falls off.</li>
        <li><strong>Dose:</strong> 2,500mg is the floor with any published support. 8,000mg to 10,000mg is the well-studied upper range.</li>
        <li><strong>Limitation:</strong> the skin data is decent, the hair and nail data is thin, and nobody should expect a change they can photograph before week eight.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

