      <h2 id="what-is-marine-collagen">What is marine collagen, exactly?</h2>

      <p>
        Marine collagen is collagen extracted from fish, usually from the skin and scales, and it is
        almost entirely type I. Collagen is the structural protein that holds skin, bone, tendon and
        blood vessel walls together, and type I is the version that dominates the dermis. So the
        source and the target tissue happen to match, which is the whole reason skin formulas
        gravitated to fish in the first place.
      </p>

      <p>
        "Fish collagen" and "marine collagen" mean the same thing on an Indian label. Most of the
        supply comes from tilapia, cod, snapper or Pacific whitefish, processed from the parts of the
        catch that would otherwise be discarded. Japanese and Korean processors dominate the
        high-grade end of that market, which is why so many premium packs specify a country of
        origin for the collagen even when the product is bottled elsewhere.
      </p>

      <p>
        Raw collagen is useless as a supplement. The intact molecule is around 300,000 Daltons, far
        too large to cross the gut wall, so it has to be enzymatically chopped into short chains
        first. That process is hydrolysis, and the output is what labels call
        <a href="/pages/collagen-peptides">collagen peptides</a>. When you see "hydrolysed marine
        collagen" or "marine collagen peptides", that is the same product described two ways.
      </p>

      <p>
        One more distinction that gets muddled in marketing copy. Type I is skin, bone and tendon.
        Type II is cartilage, and it is the one that matters for
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">joint support</a>. Marine collagen from fish skin is
        not a joint supplement, whatever the pack photograph of a running knee suggests. If you want
        the full breakdown of which type does what, we wrote
        <a href="/pages/types-of-collagen">a separate piece on collagen types</a>.
      </p>

