      <h2 id="molecular-weight">Why does molecular weight matter?</h2>

      <p>
        Because it decides whether the collagen you swallow ever reaches your bloodstream as
        collagen. Peptides below roughly 2,000 Daltons cross the intestinal wall efficiently.
        Anything meaningfully larger is broken down into loose amino acids, which your body then
        spends on whatever it happens to need that day, and that is rarely your face.
      </p>

      <p>
        The evidence that any of this survives digestion comes from Iwai and colleagues, who showed
        in 2005 that two specific fragments, Pro-Hyp and Hyp-Gly, appear measurably in human blood
        after oral collagen hydrolysate. That was the finding that moved the field. It is thought
        those fragments act less as building material and more as a signal, reaching the dermis and
        prompting fibroblasts (your collagen-making cells) to raise output.
      </p>

      <p>
        Marine collagen has a structural head start here. Fish collagen has a lower proline and
        hydroxyproline content and a less tightly wound helix than mammalian collagen, so it
        hydrolyses down to smaller fragments more readily. That is the real technical argument for
        the marine source, and it is a modest, mechanistic one rather than a dramatic one.
      </p>

      <p>
        Where I would push back on the category is the word "micro". Micro-hydrolysed, ultra-low
        molecular weight, nano peptide: none of these are regulated terms in India. Some brands mean
        a genuine sub-1,000 Da fraction. Others mean nothing at all. If the number is not printed
        anywhere on the pack or the product page, treat the adjective as decoration.
      </p>

