      <h2 id="benefits">What are the benefits of marine collagen?</h2>

      <p>
        The strongest, most consistent benefit is skin hydration, followed by elasticity, with wrinkle
        depth a distant third. The 2021 meta-analysis in the <em>International Journal of
        Dermatology</em> pooled 19 randomised controlled trials across 1,125 participants and found
        statistically significant improvement on all three measures against placebo. That is a real
        result, and it is also a modest one.
      </p>

      <p>Reading the underlying trials, the pattern is fairly reliable:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Hydration shifts first,</strong> often measurable by week four, and with the largest effect size of any endpoint.</li>
        <li><strong>Elasticity follows at eight to twelve weeks.</strong> Bolke and colleagues, testing 2.5g daily in a 2019 <em>Nutrients</em> trial, saw elasticity and dermal density improve over twelve weeks.</li>
        <li><strong>Wrinkle depth changes least.</strong> Statistically significant, visually subtle. Marine collagen is not an alternative to a clinic procedure and I would not sell it as one.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Hair and nails are where I want to be careful, because that is where the category oversells
        hardest. The hair shaft is keratin, not collagen, so the plausible mechanism is indirect:
        collagen supports the dermal environment around the follicle rather than the fibre itself.
        The trials are small, short and mostly manufacturer funded. Nails have slightly better
        support for growth rate and brittleness, from a small 2017 study, but "slightly better than
        thin" is still thin. We go through what the hair evidence does and does not cover in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-hair">the hair article</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is also a benefit that has nothing to do with collagen. Most people in India who
        report feeling a difference in the first month are describing hydration, which comes from
        hyaluronic acid and ceramides if the formula contains them, not from dermal remodelling. It
        is a genuine effect. It is just not the one on the front of the box. The
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">skin trial deep dive</a> takes that timeline apart week by
        week.
      </p>

      <h2 id="dose">How much marine collagen should you take a day?</h2>

      <p>
        Between 2.5g and 10g of hydrolysed collagen peptides daily, which is the range essentially
        every positive trial sits inside. Below 2,500mg the published literature is close to silent.
        Above 10g there is no evidence of extra benefit, only extra cost.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the number Indian buyers get caught by most often. A sachet delivering 500mg or
        1,000mg of collagen is not a small version of a studied dose; it is below the range anyone
        has tested. At 1,000mg a day you would need to take five to ten servings to reach a trial
        dose, at which point the arithmetic on price per gram stops being flattering.
      </p>

      <p>
        Timing matters far less than the marketing implies. There is no good evidence favouring
        morning over evening, empty stomach over food. What predicts results, in the trials and in
        my clinic, is whether someone actually took it every day for twelve weeks. Consistency is
        boring and it is the variable that moves.
      </p>

      <p>
        One thing does need to be present alongside the dose: vitamin C. Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase,
        the enzymes that stabilise the collagen triple helix, cannot function without it. Feed your
        body peptides with no vitamin C anywhere near them and synthesis is rate-limited no matter
        how many milligrams you paid for. Zinc plays a similar enabling role on the enzyme side.
      </p>

