      <h2 id="sustainability">Sustainability, and the bovine question</h2>

      <p>
        Marine collagen has a genuine sustainability argument, though a narrower one than the
        packaging suggests. Fish processing leaves roughly 60% of the animal as by-product: skins,
        scales, bones and frames. Collagen extraction uses that stream rather than farming anything
        additional, which is a straightforward efficiency gain.
      </p>

      <p>
        The honest caveats. Wild-caught by-product raises the same fishery questions as the catch it
        came from, and traceability in the collagen supply chain is patchy. Sustainability language
        on a bottle is not a certification. If it matters to you, look for a named species and a
        named country of origin, which is the only part of the claim that can be checked.
      </p>

      <p>
        Against bovine, the dietary point is what actually decides it for most Indian households.
        Bovine collagen is made from cow hide, which rules it out for a large share of buyers here
        for reasons that have nothing to do with absorption science. Marine collagen is
        pescatarian-friendly and, for many families, the only source on the table. Neither is vegan;
        there is no such thing as vegan collagen, only nutrients that support your own synthesis,
        which we cover in
        <a href="/pages/boost-collagen-naturally">how to boost collagen naturally</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The full comparison, including cost per gram, peptide size and where bovine legitimately
        wins, sits in <a href="/pages/marine-vs-bovine-collagen">marine vs bovine collagen</a>. Short
        version for this article: for skin, marine. For general structural support and a cheaper
        gram, bovine is a reasonable choice if your diet allows it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="indian-label">How to read an Indian marine collagen label</h2>

      <p>
        Six checks, in order of how much they change the outcome. Most packs fail at the first one,
        which saves you time.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">What to check</th><th scope="col">Green flag</th><th scope="col">Walk away</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Collagen dose per serving</th><td>Stated in mg or g. 2,500mg minimum, 8,000mg to 10,000mg is the studied upper range</td><td>"Collagen blend", no number, or a number that is the blend total rather than the collagen</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Hydrolysed and to what size</th><td>"Hydrolysed" plus a molecular weight under 2,000 Da</td><td>"Collagen" alone, or "micro" with no figure anywhere</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Source and origin</th><td>Named fish source and country of processing</td><td>"Premium imported collagen" with no species</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Vitamin C</th><td>Present in the same serving, dose printed</td><td>Absent, or listed with no quantity</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Sugar per serving</th><td>Zero, or under 2g</td><td>8g to 12g, common in collagen drinks. Sugar drives glycation, which damages the collagen you already have</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">FSSAI licence number</th><td>Printed on the pack, and matches the brand on the FSSAI portal</td><td>Missing, or a licence held by a name unrelated to the seller</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Two India-specific notes. First, the FSSAI number is worth thirty seconds of checking on
        imported-looking packs sold through marketplaces, because relabelled stock is common. Second,
        compare on price per gram of collagen, not price per pack. Divide the pack price by servings,
        then by grams of collagen per serving. A shot that looks expensive per bottle is often
        cheaper per gram than a sachet at a third of the price, because the sachet holds a fifth of
        the collagen.
      </p>

      <p>
        And the co-factor column matters more than buyers expect. Two products at the same collagen
        dose are not equivalent if one includes vitamin C, zinc and hydration agents and the other is
        collagen powder with a flavour. Since most Indian diets already run on dal, paneer and rajma
        rather than fish, the collagen itself is the unusual input; the vitamin C to use it with is
        the easy part to get right, and plenty of brands still skip it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="who-should-avoid">Who should not take it</h2>

      <p>
        Anyone with a fish or shellfish allergy should not take marine collagen, full stop. This is
        the clearest contraindication in the category and it is not a sensitivity you experiment
        with. Bovine collagen is the usual alternative for allergic patients whose diet allows it.
      </p>

      <p>Beyond that, the list of people I ask to check with their own doctor first:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pregnant and breastfeeding women.</strong> Not because collagen is known to be harmful, but because it has not been studied in pregnancy and formulas usually carry botanicals and vitamin A precursors that have not been either.</li>
        <li><strong>Anyone with kidney disease</strong> or on a protein-restricted diet, since collagen is protein and 8g a day is not nothing on a restricted budget.</li>
        <li><strong>People on prescription medication,</strong> particularly where a formula includes botanical extracts and antioxidants that may interact.</li>
        <li><strong>Children and adolescents.</strong> No meaningful data, no reason to start.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Side effects in healthy adults are mild and uncommon: a full feeling, occasional loose stools,
        sometimes a fishy aftertaste with poorly processed collagen. Nothing dramatic shows up in the
        trial safety data.
      </p>

      <p>
        The limitation I would put on our own product, since this is a buyer's guide: Collabloom's
        zinc is 1.5mg per shot, which supports the collagen enzymes but sits well below the daily
        requirement for an adult. It is a co-factor dose, not a zinc supplement, and if you are
        actually zinc deficient you need that treated separately. Same principle applies to any
        formula that lists a long ingredient panel. Read the milligrams, not the count of actives.
      </p>

