      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Is marine collagen better than bovine collagen?</summary>
          <p>
            For skin, marine has the edge. It is almost entirely type I, the dominant collagen in the
            dermis, and it hydrolyses to smaller peptides that absorb more readily. Bovine is a type
            I and III mix, usually cheaper per gram, and better suited to general structural support
            than to skin specifically.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much marine collagen should I take per day?</summary>
          <p>
            Trials showing skin benefits used 2.5g to 10g of hydrolysed collagen peptides daily.
            Anything under 2,500mg has almost no published support behind it. Collabloom delivers
            8,000mg per 30ml shot, which sits in the well-studied upper part of that range.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does marine collagen taste or smell fishy?</summary>
          <p>
            Well-processed marine collagen is close to neutral, and any residual note is usually
            covered by the flavour system. A strong fishy taste normally means poor deodorisation
            during processing rather than a stronger product. Collabloom is a wild berry flavoured
            shot, sweetened with stevia and sugar free.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take marine collagen during pregnancy?</summary>
          <p>
            Ask your obstetrician first. Collagen peptides have not been studied in pregnancy, and
            most marine collagen formulas also carry botanical extracts and antioxidants with no
            pregnancy safety data. The absence of evidence is the reason for caution, not a known
            risk.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is marine collagen suitable for vegetarians?</summary>
          <p>
            No. Marine collagen is derived from fish, so it suits pescatarians but not vegetarians or
            vegans. There is no genuine vegan collagen; products sold that way supply vitamin C,
            amino acids and other nutrients that support your own collagen synthesis rather than
            collagen itself.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does marine collagen take to work?</summary>
          <p>
            Hydration changes are often measurable around week four, elasticity at eight to twelve
            weeks, and wrinkle depth later and to a smaller degree. Judge a product at twelve weeks
            of daily use, not at three. Benefits fade over a few months if you stop, because the
            underlying age-related decline resumes.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

